Fig. 11 illustrates the local inflammatory response 2 weeks post-injection of vaccine adjuvants, comparing Nano-11 with aluminum hydroxide (AH). The hematoxylin and eosin-stained sections reveal that AH induces extensive granulomatous inflammation, characterized by dense aggregates of macrophages within the skeletal muscle. In contrast, Nano-11 injection sites exhibit significantly less residual inflammation, with relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords, indicating a more transient and less severe inflammatory reaction compared to AH.